@*
    =================================================================================================
     Routes.razor  —  the client-side router: maps the URL to a page component
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     What it is:
       Blazor's <Router> scans our assembly for components that declare  @page "/some/url"  and,
       for the current URL, renders the matching one.  Navigation between pages does NOT reload the
       browser page: the router swaps components over the existing SignalR circuit.

     What the student sees on screen: the current page, wrapped in MainLayout (header + navigation).
     Data source: none.

     Read next: Layout/MainLayout.razor, then Pages/Home.razor.
    =================================================================================================
*@
@* AppAssembly: where to look for @page components — our own project (the assembly that contains Program). *@
<Router AppAssembly="typeof(Program).Assembly">
    @* <Found> is used when a page matches the URL.  Context="routeData" names the variable that holds
       the matched page type and its route parameters. *@
    <Found Context="routeData">
        @* RouteView renders the matched page inside the default layout (MainLayout) unless the page
           chooses another layout with @layout. *@
        <RouteView RouteData="routeData" DefaultLayout="typeof(Layout.MainLayout)" />
        @* Accessibility: after each navigation, move keyboard/screen-reader focus to the page's <h1>. *@
        <FocusOnNavigate RouteData="routeData" Selector="h1" />
    </Found>
</Router>
